#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092143 :question/id #uuid "99652ff2-d3b3-5bce-a19c-dff09263aba0" :question/math-academy-id "e-930" :question/problem "How much of a $2\\%$ acid solution must be added to $300$ milliliters of a $7\\%$ acid solution to get a solution that is $5\\%$ acid?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{total}\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the number of milliliters of the $2\\%$ acid solution that must be added to the $300$ milliliters of $7\\%$ acid solution. Then there will be a total of $300+x$ milliliters of the resulting $5\\%$ acid solution.\n\nWe organize this information in a percentage–total–part table:\n\n| % Acid | Total (ml) | Part Acid (ml) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $300$ | $0.07 \\cdot 300$ |\n| $2$ | $x$ | $0.02 \\cdot x$ |\n| $5$ | $300+x$ | $0.05 \\cdot (300+x)$ |\n\nThe parts acid in each of the $7\\%$ and $2\\%$ solutions add up to the part acid in the $5\\%$ solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0.07 \\cdot 300 + 0.02 \\cdot x = 0.05 \\cdot (300+x)\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.07 \\cdot 300 + 0.02 \\cdot x &= 0.05 \\cdot (300 + x) \\\\\n21 + 0.02x &= 15 + 0.05x \\\\\n6 + 0.02x &= 0.05x \\\\\n6 &= 0.03x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $100$ allows us to solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\cdot 6 &= 100 \\cdot 0.03x \\\\\n600 &= 3x \\\\\n200 &= x \\\\\nx &= 200\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $200$ milliliters of the $2\\%$ solution must be added."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3dd3f3d-cff9-5cdd-8180-44154d5b115f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048345 :topic/id #uuid "9779346a-0ae5-5b28-ae74-23e99f006542" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068822}] :topic/math-academy-id 426 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051067}] :topic/title "Modeling With Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049200 :topic/id #uuid "e2ea8cb9-99ed-50a0-9f84-e14a154720d8" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138528}] :topic/math-academy-id 2270 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050942}] :topic/title "Solving Linear Equations With Decimal Coefficients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050716 :topic/id #uuid "60d65c05-8180-5d09-9673-b4401c622309" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147484}] :topic/math-academy-id 2453 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050776}] :topic/title "Finding Part of a Number Given a Whole and a Percentage"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af68c6cc-8429-54c9-bea4-12b0224d48eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.8$ tons and $4.2$ tons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089184} {:answer/id #uuid "10626678-2612-5de4-86f8-60685db869f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ tons and $1.5$ tons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089185} {:answer/id #uuid "2045a81b-e60b-501a-971c-2213f940888c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.6$ tons and $2.4$ tons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089186} {:answer/id #uuid "56ec6e4c-1455-5a71-b8a5-5f69a10ec88c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.2$ tons and $4.8$ tons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089187} {:answer/id #uuid "5f484ac8-0055-55d3-8fc2-67afd895bebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1.4$ tons and $6.6$ tons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56ec6e4c-1455-5a71-b8a5-5f69a10ec88c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.2$ tons and $4.8$ tons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089187} :answer-field/id #uuid "b636eee7-2b20-5cf9-9d78-36e051acd41f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089183}] :question/id #uuid "7155be1f-7c98-5780-9627-c6ea68ff27d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-10628" :question/problem "Steel is an alloy of iron and carbon. How much $55 \\mathrm{\\% }$ carbon steel and $30 \\mathrm{\\% }$ carbon steel must be mixed together to make $8$ tons of $40 \\mathrm{\\% }$ carbon steel?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{total}\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the number of tons of $55\\%$ carbon steel. Since the mixture must total $8$ tons, there will be $8-x$ tons of $30\\%$ carbon steel.\n\nWe organize this information in a percentage–total–part table:\n\n| % Carbon | Total (tons) | Part Carbon (tons) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $55$ | $x$ | $0.55x$ |\n| $30$ | $8-x$ | $0.30(8-x)$ |\n| $40$ | $8$ | $0.40\\cdot 8$ |\n\nThe parts carbon in the two starting amounts add up to the part carbon in the final mixture. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0.55x + 0.30(8-x) = 0.40\\cdot 8\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.55x + 2.4 - 0.30x &= 3.2 \\\\\n0.25x + 2.4 &= 3.2 \\\\\n0.25x &= 0.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $100$ allows us to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x &= 80 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{80}{25} \\\\\nx &= 3.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe amount of $30\\%$ carbon steel is $8-3.2=4.8$ tons.\n\nTherefore, mix **$3.2$ tons of $55\\%$ carbon steel and $4.8$ tons of $30\\%$ carbon steel**, in the order asked."}